  "finalText": "Every row in My retainers now shows its next event. Lint, typecheck and the production build all pass. I checked the logic with throwaway tests and by rendering rows with sample data, not against a wallet or the live chain.\n\n```\n$ bash scripts/check.sh\nFound 0 warnings and 0 errors.   (oxlint, type-aware: src + scripts)\nFound 0 warnings and 0 errors.   (oxlint: whole project)\n$ tsc --noEmit                   (clean)\n\n$ bun run build\ncheck-errors: all 26 ABI errors have a message.\n$ tsc --noEmit\nvite v6.4.5 building for production...\n✓ 6611 modules transformed.\ndist/assets/index-BeSPKDfz.css     18.47 kB │ gzip: 4.75 kB\ndist/assets/index-DCYAbdaY.js   1,562.85 kB │ gzip: 486.78 kB\n✓ built in 14.17s\n```\nThe only build notice is the same third-party chunk-size one as before.\n\n**How the next event works**\n- **Choice:** the next event is the earliest future one of three things: the earliest `payableAt` among Pending claims, `withdrawalReadyAt` if a withdrawal is pending, and `endTime` if the retainer hasn't ended. Something already due wins over anything in the future, because it is waiting for someone to act. If two are due, the earlier is shown.\n- **Labels:** it is labelled by kind, in bold under the row's facts:\n  - \"Claim pays out in 3m 12s\"\n  - \"Withdrawal ready in 2d 0h\"\n  - \"Ends 20 Jan 2027\"\n  - \"Claim ready to pay out\"\n  - \"Withdrawal ready to complete\"\n- **Clock:** \"in N\" and \"due\" use the shared chain-time hook. A claim whose `payableAt` equals chain time counts as due. A past end date is not \"due\" (it only gets a label while still ahead). An ended retainer with nothing scheduled shows no next event.\n- **Reads:** claims are read only for rows that are on screen and report `pendingClaims > 0`.\n  - They are read 20 at a time, newest first, because pending claims are mostly recent.\n  - Reading stops as soon as the number of Pending claims found equals `pendingClaims`.\n  - The result is keyed on the retainer's `claimCount` and `pendingClaims`, so it is read again only when one of them changes, and it does not poll on its own.\n- **Loading and failure:** while claims are being read the row says \"Checking what happens next…\". If the read fails it keeps whatever else is scheduled and says the next claim time couldn't be read. A failed claim read is never shown as \"no claims\".\n\n**Throwaway checks, then deleted:** 29 checks, 28 passed. The one failure was a mistake in my test: I typed the wrong expected date for \"Ends 20 Jan 2027\". The code was right. They covered two parts:\n- **Page walk:**\n  - 50 claims with 3 pending spread out: three pages read, then stop.\n  - Only the newest claim pending: a single read.\n  - 20 of 20 pending: one page.\n  - Fewer than a page of claims: one read of the exact length.\n  - The only pending claim being the oldest: every page, then stop at claim 0.\n  - No pending claims: zero reads.\n  - A pending counter that disagrees with the claims: stops when they run out.\n- **Event choice:** each kind, due versus future, both due, ended, and the boundary at `payableAt == now`.\n\nI did not touch `contracts/`, `deployments/`, `tracing.ts`, `console-capture.ts`, `README.md` or `AGENTS.md`, and I wrote no mainnet config.",
